Google today <a href="https://blog.google/products-and-platforms/devices/pixel/google-pixel-tag/">introduced the Pixel Tag</a>, its answer to Apple's <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/airtag/">AirTag</a>. Pixel Tag is Google's first item tracker, even though Google implemented an item tracking network two years ago that's been used by third-party devices.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/08/google-pixel-tag-design.jpg" alt="" width="1739" height="978" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1059446" /><br/>
The Pixel Tag is made from stainless steel, and it is IP67 water- and dust-resistant. There's a built-in speaker that can play a sound when an item it's attached to is lost, and a button that causes a sound to play on a connected smartphone. An on-device button is one feature that distinguishes the Pixel Tag from the &zwnj;AirTag&zwnj;.<br/>
<br/>
Pixel Tags have an oval shape instead of a round shape, and they don't come with an attachment point and like the &zwnj;AirTag&zwnj;, require an add-on accessory for adding to keys or bags. Like the &zwnj;AirTag&zwnj;, the Pixel Tag uses a replaceable battery that lasts for up to one year. It works with Android devices running Android 9 or newer with Bluetooth 6, and can be tracked in the Find Hub app.<br/>
<br/>
Google added Bluetooth and Ultra Wideband to the Pixel Tag, so it supports a precision finding feature when an item is lost nearby but out of sight. The Pixel Tag supports many of the same functions as the &zwnj;AirTag&zwnj;, like alerts when an item is left behind and the option to share item location with multiple people.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/08/google-pixel-tag-size.jpg" alt="" width="1999" height="1124" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1059447" /><br/>
The &zwnj;AirTag&zwnj; relies on Apple's <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/find-my/">Find My</a> network when an item is out of range of its owner's device, and Google has a similar system. The Find Hub network taps into more than a billion Android devices for locating items.<br/>
<br/>
Google has the same protections as Apple, and Android devices send alerts when an unknown tag is detected traveling with you. Unknown tracker alerts are cross-platform thanks to a collaboration between Apple and Google, so iPhone users will get an alert if a Pixel Tag is nearby and vice versa.<br/>
<br/>
